stianraschka.com/p/the-big-llm-architecture-comparison">architecture compar=
ison article</a><span>, I framed GQA as the new standard replacement for cl=
assic multi-head attention (MHA). The reason is that standard MHA gives eve=
ry head its own keys and values, which is more optimal from a modeling pers=
pective but expensive once we have to keep all of that state in the </span>=
<a href=3D"https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/coding-the-kv-cache-in-l=
lms?utm_source=3Dpublication-search">KV cache</a><span> during inference.</=
span></p><p><span>In GQA, we keep a larger set of query heads, but we reduc=
e the number of key-value heads and let multiple queries share them. That l=
owers both parameter count and </span><a href=3D"https://magazine.sebastian=
raschka.com/p/coding-the-kv-cache-in-llms?utm_source=3Dpublication-search">=
KV-cache</a><span> traffic without making drastic implementation changes li=
ke multi-head latent attention (MLA), which will be discussed later. </span=
></p><p>In practice, that made and keeps it a very popular choice for labs =
that wanted something cheaper than MHA but simpler to implement than newer =
compression-heavy alternatives like MLA.</p><h2 class=3D"header-anchor-post=
">2.2 GQA Memory Savings<div class=3D"pencraft pc-display-flex pc-alignItem=
